<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hauran</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hawran, Auranitis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hauran is a volcanic plateau and fertile plain in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, extending into northern Jordan, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golan-heights">Golan Heights</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabal-al-druze">Jabal al-Druze</a>. Long a granary for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, it was ruled in turn by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaeans">Aramaeans</a>, Nabataeans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and Ghassanids, and its black basalt towns, such as Bosra, preserve many ancient buildings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hauran.jpg" alt="Hauran" width="320" height="288" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Amitchell125 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Hauran_(satellite_version).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Druze moved east to the <b>Hauran</b> in waves from 1685, and after the battle of Ain Dara in 1711 many Yamani Druze followed, laying the foundation of the Jabal al-Druze, while Maronites became the dominant group in Mount Lebanon.</blockquote><a href="/religion/druze-faith">Druze faith · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Its kings ruled from Damascus over the oasis and the <b>Hauran</b>, controlling caravan routes from Arabia.</blockquote><a href="/state/aram-damascus">Aram-Damascus</a></li><li><blockquote>Duqaq's emirate covered Damascus, the <b>Hawran</b> and parts of the Beqaa.</blockquote><a href="/state/seljuk-emirate-of-damascus">Seljuk Emirate of Damascus</a></li><li><blockquote>Philip received the lands north and east of the Sea of Galilee: Gaulanitis, Batanaea, Trachonitis, <b>Auranitis</b> and part of Ituraea, a frontier zone the Romans had entrusted to Herod to suppress brigandage.</blockquote><a href="/state/tetrarchy-of-philip">Tetrarchy of Philip</a></li></ul></section>
